WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.051
Picture a café owner with a shoebox of paper receipts, one for every card payment.

2
00:00:08.051 --> 00:00:13.401
Each receipt has an id, the shop, the currency, a status, the amount and the time.

3
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.999
To add up the amounts, she used to read every receipt.

4
00:00:16.999 --> 00:00:23.167
So she cut each one into strips, one per detail, and filed them in six binders, in the same order.

5
00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:25.119
Now the total needs one binder.

6
00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:27.979
But open the currency binder, and look.

7
00:00:27.979 --> 00:00:29.648
EUR, EUR, EUR, GBP, EUR.

8
00:00:29.648 --> 00:00:33.613
A million strips, and only four different values on them.

9
00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:36.395
So how small can a column like this get?

10
00:00:36.395 --> 00:00:37.716
Hold that question.

11
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:40.972
Take twelve strips, one chunk of the column.

12
00:00:40.972 --> 00:00:45.184
Space is counted in bytes, and a byte holds about one letter.

13
00:00:45.184 --> 00:00:51.329
Stored plainly, each value is a 4-byte length, then 3 bytes of text: 84 bytes for twelve.

14
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.666
A million take 7 MB, for four different values.

15
00:00:54.666 --> 00:01:00.594
Write E U R by hand a million times, and by strip four hundred you'd invent a shorthand.

16
00:01:00.594 --> 00:01:04.299
That's the wish: a shorthand that's quick to read back.

17
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:10.394
The first idea most of us have: zip it, the way you zip files before you email them.

18
00:01:10.394 --> 00:01:11.447
It does shrink.

19
00:01:11.633 --> 00:01:21.539
But every scan then pays to unzip whatever it touches, and to read one value, you unzip the whole block around it, about 256 KB.

20
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:27.275
It's a vacuum-packed binder: small on the shelf, but you cut open the bag to read one strip.

21
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:29.437
So back to the shorthand.

22
00:01:29.437 --> 00:01:38.043
On the binder's cover, the owner writes a key, in alphabetical order: 0 is EUR, 1 is GBP, 2 is SEK, and 3 is USD.

23
00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:41.693
Now each strip holds one small number instead of a word.

24
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:42.738
Let's map it.

25
00:01:42.738 --> 00:01:45.252
The key on the cover is the dictionary.

26
00:01:45.252 --> 00:01:49.249
The number on a strip is a code: its value's place in the key.

27
00:01:49.249 --> 00:01:51.505
This is called dictionary encoding.

28
00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:58.680
And since the key is sorted, a test like currency before GBP becomes code below 1, right on the codes.

29
00:01:58.933 --> 00:02:01.755
Here the binders stop being a good picture.

30
00:02:01.755 --> 00:02:06.152
A paper strip with a 0 on it is just as thick as one that says EUR.

31
00:02:06.152 --> 00:02:08.383
Paper doesn't shrink, but bits do.

32
00:02:08.567 --> 00:02:12.345
A bit is a single 0 or 1, and 8 bits make a byte.

33
00:02:12.345 --> 00:02:16.047
Our codes only go up to 3, and 3 fits in 2 bits.

34
00:02:16.233 --> 00:02:19.331
So how many bytes do the twelve codes take now?

35
00:02:19.331 --> 00:02:20.385
Pause and guess.

36
00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:28.120
Two bits each, written back to back: 24 bits, which is 3 bytes.

37
00:02:28.333 --> 00:02:32.443
With the key, the chunk takes 31 bytes instead of 84.

38
00:02:32.443 --> 00:02:36.242
A million currencies take 250 KB instead of 7 MB.

39
00:02:36.242 --> 00:02:42.988
Storing each number in the fewest bits that hold the largest one is called bit-packing.

40
00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:45.281
Numbers get the same trick.

41
00:02:45.281 --> 00:02:52.349
Twelve amounts, in cents, each in an 8-byte slot built for numbers with nineteen digits: 96 bytes.

42
00:02:52.533 --> 00:02:58.002
First, subtract the chunk's smallest amount, 300, from every value.

43
00:02:58.002 --> 00:03:02.573
Now the largest left is 8,600, and that fits in 14 bits.

44
00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.807
Twelve values take 168 bits.

45
00:03:04.807 --> 00:03:12.105
In three 8-byte words, plus a small header that remembers the 300, that's 40 bytes instead of 96.

46
00:03:12.105 --> 00:03:16.544
Taking off a base like this is called a frame of reference.

47
00:03:16.767 --> 00:03:18.057
One more shorthand.

48
00:03:18.057 --> 00:03:25.868
If the table were sorted by currency, equal codes would sit together: eight zeros, two ones, one two and one three.

49
00:03:26.067 --> 00:03:28.676
Write each run once, with its length.

50
00:03:28.676 --> 00:03:29.382
Four runs.

51
00:03:29.382 --> 00:03:31.286
That's run-length encoding.

52
00:03:31.286 --> 00:03:33.261
It shines on sorted columns.

53
00:03:33.467 --> 00:03:35.532
So should every column use runs?

54
00:03:35.532 --> 00:03:38.759
Try it on our twelve amounts, which aren't sorted.

55
00:03:38.759 --> 00:03:40.695
Smaller than plain, or bigger?

56
00:03:40.695 --> 00:03:41.728
Pause and guess.

57
00:03:44.967 --> 00:03:45.605
Bigger.

58
00:03:45.605 --> 00:03:52.262
Twelve runs of length one, at 12 bytes each: 144 bytes, against 96 plain.

59
00:03:52.262 --> 00:03:53.904
Not much of a run.

60
00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.144
So no encoding wins everywhere.

61
00:03:56.144 --> 00:04:02.540
For each chunk, the engine tries them all and keeps the smallest: here, bit-packing, at 40 bytes.

62
00:04:02.733 --> 00:04:07.808
DuckDB, a free database built for reports, does this chunk by chunk.

63
00:04:07.808 --> 00:04:15.717
On the lesson's million payments, it bit-packed the numbers and gave the text a dictionary: 5.5 MB in all.

64
00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:20.813
PostgreSQL, a free database that keeps whole rows together, needs 66.

65
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:23.623
Disks are cheap, so why squeeze at all?

66
00:04:23.623 --> 00:04:30.159
Because a scan spends most of its time moving bytes, from the disk to memory and on to the processor.

67
00:04:30.333 --> 00:04:39.535
250 KB is about 28 times less to move than 7 MB, and undoing a 2-bit code takes a few simple steps.

68
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:41.904
So, how small can a column get?

69
00:04:41.904 --> 00:04:44.144
As small as its values are alike.

70
00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:46.951
Write each different value once, in a key.

71
00:04:46.951 --> 00:04:49.227
Store each number in the fewest bits.

72
00:04:49.227 --> 00:04:51.319
Write a run once, with its length.

73
00:04:51.319 --> 00:04:53.780
Then try them all and keep the smallest.

74
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.088
One quick check.

75
00:04:55.088 --> 00:04:59.373
Ten million payments, sorted by status, which has three values.

76
00:04:59.373 --> 00:05:02.773
Which shorthand stores the status column smallest?

77
00:05:02.773 --> 00:05:03.861
Pause and think.

78
00:05:07.033 --> 00:05:07.442
Runs.

79
00:05:07.442 --> 00:05:15.040
Sorted by status, almost every chunk holds one status: a single run, one value and one count.

80
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.533
Bit-packed codes would still cost 2 bits a row, 2.5 MB.

81
00:05:19.733 --> 00:05:25.504
Each chunk also notes its smallest and largest value, so a scan can skip it unread.

82
00:05:25.504 --> 00:05:26.339
That's next.
